From 7ce026928479df337314d3d4f3e28bda87bd8229 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 14:41:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] fix(api): close the open pipeline, and make a job status
 mean something
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects from FLOORFORGE_SYSTEM_BASELINE.md §3.4 and §3.5.

1. GET /api/applications returned every pilot lead — name, email, phone,
   company, sqft target, internal notes — to anyone who asked. Its own comment
   said "For now, this is open for demo purposes." /operator/* was never in
   proxy.ts's protected matcher either, so neither the console nor the API
   behind it was covered.

   lib/apiAuth.ts guards it, and fails closed: with no identity provider
   configured these routes answer 503, never data. The repository's principle
   that the site deploys with zero env vars is right for the marketing site;
   "no auth configured" must still mean the operator API is unavailable, not
   that it is open. The API routes guard themselves in the handler rather than
   in middleware, because a middleware match redirects to a sign-in page — the
   right answer for a page, the wrong one for a fetch.

2. PATCH /api/jobs/[id] hand-rolled an allowlist and wrote `status` from the
   body with no enum check, although validateJobUpdate had existed at
   lib/validators.ts:248 the whole time as dead code. And 001 wrote out nine
   status transition rules as a comment while enforcing one of them, so a job
   could reach `approved` without ever having run. A completion report is a
   commercial document; if `approved` can be set from `draft`, every report,
   warranty and pilot metric derived from it means nothing.

   lib/jobState.ts holds the table, checked here for a 409 that names what was
   allowed and again by the trigger in migration 002 §6, which holds under
   concurrency and holds for callers who never came through this file.

3. GET /api/jobs/[id] called getJobById(id) with one argument although the
   helper takes tenant_id, so any job id read any job in any tenant.

   Now scoped — and labelled in the file as scoping, not authorisation. It
   narrows what a request touches; it does not verify the caller belongs to
   that tenant, because `users` has no column linking a row to a session. That
   gap is the next mission, not a thing to paper over.

Two smaller ones found along the way: the public waitlist validator copied
attacker-supplied `internal_notes` into the field the operator console shows as
staff commentary, and isValidJobStatus kept a second copy of the status list —
the same duplicate-enum pattern that made telemetry unstorable.

